WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.232 --> 00:00:01.440
MAX SALTONSTALL: [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.450
LORIN PRICE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.160
MAX SALTONSTALL: Wait a second.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.300
What is your role, again?

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.410
LORIN PRICE: I'm a wizard.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.030
See, I have a wand.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.060
MAX SALTONSTALL: I thought I
was the wizard in this party.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.220
Can we clarify our roles
a little bit before we get

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.660
any deeper into this dungeon?

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.060
LORIN PRICE: You know,
keeping track of custom roles

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.393
on GCP using Deployment Manager
is so much easier than this.

00:00:21.393 --> 00:00:23.310
MAX SALTONSTALL: Great,
let's do that instead.

00:00:30.555 --> 00:00:32.820
LORIN PRICE: To build
this yourself, first,

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.830
you must make sure that
the Deployment Manager

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:38.190
API and the Identity and
Access Management API

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.770
are enabled in your API library.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.950
If they aren't, enable both,
and give it a few minutes

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:46.470
to propagate before
trying this out.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:50.310
Next, if you want to follow
along with the exact demo,

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:52.230
you can clone the
contents from the GitHub

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.175
repo linked in the description.

00:00:56.175 --> 00:00:58.050
I want to start by
showing you the roles that

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.090
already exist in my project.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.480
It's pretty minimal,
and most are predefined.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.620
When we're finished, we'll have
a new customer all listed here.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.430
Let's move on to explore the
contents of this GitHub repo

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.590
I've cloned in the Cloud Shell.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.780
You'll see three
different types of files

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:19.810
each existing at the project
and organization levels.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.380
Let's dig into the project level
for the purposes of this demo.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:25.590
The yaml file is
called a configuration.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.840
Configurations describe
resources and their properties,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.052
such as the name,
description, and permissions.

00:01:32.052 --> 00:01:33.510
This is what will
hold the customer

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.640
all permissions for the demo.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.190
The second file
type is the schema.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:41.070
These files specify
all possible properties

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.590
for a given resource and
their default values.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.260
Finally, there are
templates, which

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.110
can be Jinja or Python files.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.360
Templates enhance
configuration files

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.870
by allowing you to break
it down into reusable parts

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:58.140
for the Deployment Manager.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.420
So let's go ahead and
create a deployment.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.780
I'll use the yaml config file
to create a custom role that

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.730
gives me permissions
to manage all

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.770
of the descriptions
of the spells

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.560
that I know in
Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.000
We'll give it a
minute to finish,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.770
and if all goes
according to plan,

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:18.330
we should see the completed
state with no errors.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:20.940
I can now go into
the IAM console,

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.030
jumping down to roles,
and sort by custom.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.350
If I click into the
role that was created,

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.170
I can see the permissions
that it was given

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.167
in the yaml configuration file.

00:02:30.167 --> 00:02:31.750
MAX SALTONSTALL:
That all seems great,

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.398
but what if I level up and want
to make changes to the role

00:02:34.398 --> 00:02:35.190
that I've deployed?

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.380
LORIN PRICE: Funny that you ask.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.200
Let's jump back into
our yaml configuration.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.620
I'm going to change our stage
from EAP to alpha and add

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.640
a new permission, so now
I can upload objects.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.160
Instead of creating
another new deployment,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.430
I'm going to update my
existing deployment referencing

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.580
the same configuration file.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.360
Back in the console, we can
refresh the IAM role page,

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.970
and see right away
that the role stage has

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.440
been upgraded to alpha.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.612
And our extra
permission was added.

00:03:06.612 --> 00:03:08.820
MAX SALTONSTALL: That really
makes this process super

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.350
easy for managing custom roles.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.720
I finally understand
who can do what job.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.350
LORIN PRICE: It really does.

00:03:14.350 --> 00:03:15.870
And the great thing
is if a person

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.210
is assigned a custom
role, changes to the role

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.130
take effect as soon as
the role is updated.

